def is_prime(n: int) -> bool:
    """Check whether a number is prime."""
    if n < 2:
        return False
    if n == 2:
        return True
    if n % 2 == 0:
        return False
    for i in range(3, int(n**0.5) + 1, 2):
        if n % i == 0:
            return False
    return True
```

Here are the most common ways to center a `div` using CSS, depending on your specific needs:

### 1. Center Both Horizontally and Vertically (Flexbox)
This is the most modern and reliable method. Apply it to the **parent** container:

```css
.parent {
  display: flex;
  justify-content: center; /* Centers horizontally */
  align-items: center;     /* Centers vertically */
}
```

### 2. Center Horizontally Only (Margin Auto)
Apply this to the **div itself**. The div must have a defined width:

```css
.div {
  margin-left: auto;
  margin-right: auto;
  /* Or simply: */
  margin: 0 auto;
}
```

### 3. Center Both Horizontally and Vertically (Grid)
Apply this to the **parent** container:

```css
.parent {
  display: grid;
  place-items: center; /* Centers both horizontally and vertically */
}
```

### 4. Center Both Horizontally and Vertically (Absolute Positioning)
Useful for overlays or modals. Apply to the **div itself**:

```css
.div {
  position: absolute;
  top: 50%;
  left: 50%;
  transform: translate(-50%, -50%);
}
```

**Recommendation:** Use **Flexbox** (Option 1) or **Grid** (Option 3) for most cases, as they are simpler and more robust.
